is better in case I can get another.\\n\\nLooking at the melds of other players: The next player has Pungs of J1 and W4. The player across has Pungs of J3 and B1-2-3. The previous player has a Pung of W2. So they might be focusing on different suits. Maybe I can focus on forming another Chow or Pung in my own suits.\\n\\nIf I can form another Chow in the T suit. For example, T5-6-7. I have T5. If I can get T6 and T7, that would work. The unshown T6 is 3, T7 is 4. So possible. If I keep T5, maybe I can get T6 and T7. Alternatively, if I have T9s, maybe a Pung of T9. But need three, and I have two.\\n\\nSo, considering all this, which tile should I discard to maximize my chances? Let's think about which tile is the safest to discard without helping others. If I play T5, that's part of the existing meld, but since it's already in the meld, maybe it's safe. But if I discard T5, someone might need it for a Chi. However, since my meld is already there, maybe they can't Chi it. Wait, no, if another player discards a T4 or T6, I could Chi. But in this case, I'm the one discarding. So if I discard T5, it's part of my meld, but if someone else has a T4 or T6, they might be able to Chi. But since my meld is already formed, maybe they can't. Not sure.\\n\\nAlternatively, if I discard W6, which I have two of. Since there are two left in the wall, it's possible someone is trying to get it. But if I discard one, maybe it's a safe play. Also, keeping one W6 might help in forming a Pung if I can get another. But with only two left, it's not guaranteed.\\n\\nAnother angle: the current hand has a pair? Let me check. My hand tiles are W5, W6 (two), W9, T3, T5, T9 (two). So possible pairs could be W6 (if I have two) or T9 (if I have two). If I discard one of the W6 or T9, I can form a pair. For example, if I discard one W6, then I have one W6 left, and maybe a pair of T9. Or if I discard one T9, then I have one T9 left and a pair of W6. Alternatively, if I discard T5, I have T3, T9, W5,