n't contribute much unless I get another F2 for a pair.\n\n- Play T9: Similar to T8, T9 is a terminal tile. If someone has T7, T8, they might need T9 for a chow. But again, I don't have T7 or T8 (except one T8). So maybe safer to discard.\n\n- Play W6: W6 is a middle tile. If someone has W5 and W7, they might need W6. But W5 has 0 left, so maybe not. Discarding W6 might be safe.\n\n- Play W9: I have two W9s. If I discard one, someone might take it for a pung. But if I keep both, I can form a pair. Since I need a pair, keeping both might be better.\n\nSo, considering all options, discarding F2 seems the safest because it's a single honor tile that's less likely to be useful to others. By discarding F2, I maintain the possibility of forming a pung of T3 or W9, and I can work towards completing the hand with a pair of W9s or T3s.\n\nAdditionally, the F2 is a South Wind, which isn't my seat wind (West), so having it doesn't contribute to the Seat Wind pattern. Therefore, getting rid of it allows me to focus on higher-value patterns.\n\nSo the best action is to play F2.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The safest discard is F2 (South Wind), as it's a single honor tile with low utility for others and doesn't contribute to high-scoring patterns like Seat Wind (West) or Prevalent Wind (East). Keeping T3s and W9s preserves opportunities for pungs and pairs.\nReason: Discarding F2 minimizes risk of others completing melds while maintaining flexibility to form high-value patterns (e.g., pung of T3 or W9). The hand already has two chows and could develop into All Chows or All Simples with proper tile management.\nAnswer: Play F2"}
